commonMistakes:
  - title: "Using CBSE's 9.5 rule on a 4.0 or 5.0 scale"
    description: "The 9.5 multiplier was designed for CBSE's 10-point grading. Multiplying a 3.4 CGPA by 9.5 gives 32.3%, which is meaningless. On 4.0 and 5.0 scales, use the simple proportion or your university's own formula."
  - title: "Assuming one national formula"
    description: "There is no single CGPA to percentage formula that every university accepts. In Pakistan, HEC does not publish one conversion for every university, and Indian universities also set their own. The conversion printed on your transcript, or given by your examination department, is the official one."
  - title: "Dividing by the wrong scale"
    description: "A 3.5 CGPA is 87.5% of a 4.0 scale but only 70% of a 5.0 scale. Check the maximum CGPA on your transcript before converting."
  - title: "Rounding the CGPA before converting"
    description: "Round only the final percentage. Turning 3.46 into 3.5 first adds a full percentage point on a 4.0 scale (86.5% versus 87.5%)."
  - title: "Reversing with a different formula"
    description: "If you converted a CGPA to a percentage with one formula, convert back with the same one. Mixing the simple proportion with a university formula gives a CGPA that matches neither."
faqs:
  - q: "How do I convert CGPA to percentage?"
    a: "Divide your CGPA by the maximum of your scale and multiply by 100. On a 4.0 scale, 3.2 ÷ 4.0 × 100 = 80%. If your transcript or university prints its own conversion formula, use that one instead, because it is the official conversion for your degree."
  - q: "What is 3.5 CGPA in percentage?"
    a: "On a 4.0 scale with the simple proportion method, 3.5 CGPA is 87.5%. On a 5.0 scale it is 70%. Your university may use a different formula, so check your transcript before quoting the figure on an application."
  - q: "How do you convert CGPA to percentage in CBSE?"
    a: "Multiply the CGPA by 9.5. A CGPA of 8.0 becomes 76%, and to go back, divide the percentage by 9.5. This rule only applies to CBSE's 10-point scale."
  - q: "Does HEC have an official CGPA to percentage formula?"
    a: "HEC does not publish one conversion formula for every university in Pakistan. Universities set their own grading and conversion, which is usually printed on the transcript or available from the university's examination department. Enter that formula in this calculator to get your official equivalent."
  - q: "How do I convert percentage to CGPA?"
    a: "Use the same formula in reverse. With the simple proportion, CGPA = percentage ÷ 100 × scale, so 75% on a 4.0 scale is 3.0. For CBSE, divide by 9.5. For a transcript formula of the form m × CGPA + c, CGPA = (percentage − c) ÷ m."
  - q: "Why do different websites give different percentages for the same CGPA?"
    a: "They use different formulas. Some use the simple proportion, some use CBSE's 9.5 rule, and some use one university's conversion. Only the formula your own institution uses gives the official answer."
  - q: "Is CGPA the same as GPA?"
    a: "Not quite. GPA usually describes one semester or term, while CGPA is the cumulative average across all semesters so far. Both are converted to a percentage the same way. To calculate a GPA from grades and credit hours, use the GPA Calculator."
