    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="which-formula-heading">
      <h2 id="which-formula-heading">Which conversion formula should I use?</h2>
      <p>A CGPA and a percentage measure the same performance on two different scales, and there is more than one accepted way to translate between them. The right one depends on who issued your grades and who is asking for the percentage.</p>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li><strong>Your transcript's own formula:</strong> if your degree, mark sheet or transcript prints a conversion, use it. It is the figure your institution will confirm if an employer or admissions office checks.</li>
        <li><strong>CBSE's 10-point rule:</strong> for CBSE school results on the 10-point scale, multiply the CGPA by 9.5.</li>
        <li><strong>Simple proportion:</strong> when no official formula is given, dividing by the scale and multiplying by 100 is the most neutral estimate, because it treats your CGPA as a share of the maximum. Say that it is an estimate when you quote it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Whichever method you use, convert back with the same one, and round only the final answer.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="university-formula-heading">
      <h2 id="university-formula-heading">Finding your university's formula</h2>
      <p>Many universities in Pakistan and India print their own CGPA to percentage conversion on the transcript, often on the back of the page alongside the grading key, or publish it through the examination department or controller of examinations. It usually looks like <code>Percentage = m × CGPA + c</code>, where m is a multiplier and c an offset that can be zero or negative.</p>
      <p>Copy those two numbers into the "My university's formula" option. The calculator applies them exactly as written and solves the same equation backwards for percentage to CGPA. If you cannot find a formula on your documents, ask your examination department in writing; a written answer from them carries more weight than any online calculator.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="hec-heading">
      <h2 id="hec-heading">HEC and CGPA conversion in Pakistan</h2>
      <p>The Higher Education Commission does not publish one CGPA to percentage formula for every university in Pakistan. Universities set their own grading schemes, and the official conversion for a student is the one on their transcript or provided by their university's examination department. That is why this calculator has no built-in university or HEC preset: any preset added later must point to an official source for that specific university.</p>
      <p>When a job advert or scholarship form asks for a percentage and your university has not given a formula, enter the simple proportion and note that it is an estimate, or attach your transcript so the reader can see the original CGPA and scale.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="scales-heading">
      <h2 id="scales-heading">4.0, 5.0 and 10.0 CGPA scales</h2>
      <p>The scale is the highest CGPA your system awards. Most Pakistani universities and many international universities grade on 4.0, some institutions use 5.0, and CBSE and many Indian universities use a 10-point scale. The same CGPA means very different things on each: 3.5 is 87.5% of a 4.0 scale but 70% of a 5.0 scale, so always check the maximum shown on your transcript. To turn exam marks into a percentage first, use the <a href="/tools/marks-percentage-calculator/">Marks Percentage Calculator</a>, and to work out a GPA from course grades and credit hours, use the <a href="/tools/gpa-calculator/">GPA Calculator</a>.</p>
    </section>
